A second rendered review — this time of the Azure-mode removal — independently reproduced the findings here and added detail worth recording, since Standard chat is now the *only* chat journey.

**Phone width, confirmed with measurements.** `chat.tsx` uses a fixed `w-[650px]`; at 390×844 the panel renders at `left: -308px`, so message text is clipped mid-sentence ("…would suggest the", "…weather-resistant, and quick") and the product-links list is entirely off-screen.

**The reset control is not a control.** Rendered, it is a bare `<svg>` whose parent is a `div` — no interactive ancestor, no `tabindex`, no `role`, no accessible name, and a 20×20 box, under the 24×24 WCAG 2.2 minimum.

**Keyboard reachability is worse than first reported.** The chat launcher is the **24th and last** tabbable element on the home page, behind every product link. Opening the panel moves focus nowhere, so the next Tab continues into the page *behind* the panel. No `role="dialog"`, no focus trap, no Escape-to-close.

**Send button has no accessible name.** Rendered as `(no name)`; only the menu and close buttons carry labels.

**Contrast passes**, measured from resolved RGB: assistant bubble 6.08:1, user bubble 5.33:1, in-bubble product links 5.92:1, input text 10.44:1, hero copy 7.11:1.

## Two new items for this issue

**Whitespace-only messages are accepted.** The guard is `if (message === "") return;` with no trim, so three spaces and Enter fires a real request and renders an empty blue bubble.

**The home page has no headings at all.** A query for `h1..h6` returns empty — category names are `div.text-5xl`. No `h1`, so heading navigation and landmark structure are unavailable.
